<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ermine Street</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ermine Street was a major Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> running north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> through Lincoln to York, built in the early years after the conquest of 43 CE. It carried troops and supplies to the northern frontier and remained a main route for centuries; much of its line is followed by the modern A1, A10 and A15 roads. Its name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> in origin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ermine-street.jpg" alt="Ermine Street" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marek69 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newport_Arch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
